- Minimum history: `[6, 3, 3]`
- `getMin()` returns `3`

The methods are normally called with a non-empty stack for `top`, `pop`, and `getMin`, as expected by the platform.

```java
import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Deque;

class MinStack {
    private Deque<Integer> values;
    private Deque<Integer> minimums;

    public MinStack() {
        values = new ArrayDeque<>();
        minimums = new ArrayDeque<>();
    }

    public void push(int val) {
        values.push(val);
        if (minimums.isEmpty()) {
            minimums.push(val);
        } else {
            minimums.push(Math.min(val, minimums.peek()));
        }
    }

    public void pop() {
        values.pop();
        minimums.pop();
    }

    public int top() {
        return values.peek();
    }

    public int getMin() {
        return minimums.peek();
    }
}
```

`push`, `pop`, `top`, and `getMin` all run in O(1) time. The extra minimum stack means O(n) space. Duplicate minimum values are stored too, so popping one copy does not lose the answer.

A pair keeps the value and the smallest value seen up to that point. When an item is removed, the next pair already has the correct minimum, so no scan is needed.

For example, pushing 5, 2, and 4 stores `(5, 5)`, `(2, 2)`, and `(4, 2)`. Removing 4 exposes 2 as the minimum again.

